 Creating an Application in SalesForce.com using Apex programming Language
Step No 1: Create new org: 
https://developer.salesforce.com/signup 
Step No 2: After signup, logging using following URL https://login.salesforce.com/ 
Step No 3: Login Page (Enter your credential to login) 
1. Open Developer Console 
2. File ---> New ---> Select Apex Class 
3. Type LoginApp as apex class
Code:
public class LoginApp {
    public static void login(String username, String password) {
        if(username == 'admin' && password == 'pgmcoe123') {
            System.debug('Login Successful! Welcome to the Application.');
        } else {
            System.debug('Invalid Credentials. Please try again.');
        }
    }
}
Step:4 save code
Step:5 Click on Debug → Open Execute Anonymous Window
Paste this code:
LoginApp.login('admin', 'pgmcoe123');
*You can also try incorrect credentials:
LoginApp.login('user', 'wrongpass'); 
Step :6 Click Execute → Open Log → Click "Debug Only"
You’ll see:
For correct credentials:
Login Successful! Welcome to the Application.
*For wrong credentials:
Invalid Credentials. Please try again.  

Theory:
Theory: Creating a Login Application in Salesforce using Apex
In Salesforce, Apex is a strongly-typed, object-oriented programming language that allows developers to write custom business logic. Using Apex, we can simulate simple applications like a Login Page for learning and demonstration purposes.
In this example, we are creating a basic Login functionality using a static method in an Apex class. This simulation checks if the user credentials match predefined values and displays an appropriate message.

📝 Steps Explained:
Create an Apex Class (LoginApp)
The class contains a static method login(String username, String password) that accepts two parameters.
Implement Login Logic
Inside the method, we compare the input username and password with hardcoded values (admin and pgmcoe123). If they match, it displays a "Login Successful" message using System.debug. Otherwise, it shows an "Invalid Credentials" message.
Execute Using Developer Console
The method is called from the Execute Anonymous Window in the Developer Console using:
LoginApp.login('admin', 'pgmcoe123');
View Output in Logs
After execution, the result is displayed in the logs (Debug Only), simulating a basic login system.



